## Statement

Compute
$$\lim_{x\to 0}\frac{\sin x - x}{x^3}.$$

## Hint

Use the Taylor expansion of $\sin$ at $0$ up to order $3$.

## Solution

As $x\to0$, $\sin x = x-\dfrac{x^3}{6}+o(x^3)$.

Hence $\dfrac{\sin x-x}{x^3}=-\dfrac16+o(1)$, and the limit is $-\dfrac16$.
